<!-- Section: Double Integrals Over General Regions and Area by Double     -->
<!-- Integration                                                           -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Skeletal (fill-in) version of the section of the same name in the     -->
<!-- completed MATH 14 notes.  The two region types, the area formula and  -->
<!-- the reverse-order remark are kept; the four worked examples and the   -->
<!-- two Concept Check answers are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each -->
<!-- preceded by a one-line instruction.  File name and xml:ids are        -->
<!-- unchanged.                                                            -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Figures: the two region diagrams with their strips, the strip video,  -->
<!-- the tetrahedron, the reverse-order picture, the symmetry picture of   -->
<!-- Method 2, the area region, and the two GeoGebra/PreFigure statements  -->
<!-- of the Concept Check are kept.  The base of the tetrahedron, which is -->
<!-- the region of integration the student has to find, becomes an empty  -->
<!-- grid to sketch on.  Captions that stated a numerical answer have been -->
<!-- trimmed.                                                              -->
<section xml:id="section-double-integrals-general-regions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Double Integrals Over General Regions and Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
Here we will generalize our last session's discussion about double
integrals and how they can be used to evaluate volumes. Last session we
restricted ourselves to shapes with rectangular base. Today we will
look at more general shapes for the base.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case1">
<title>Case I: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : a \le x \le b,\ f_2(x) \le y \le f_1(x)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case1-vertical">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R \left(2xy + 1\right)\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 1,\ (-2x^2+2x) \le y \le (x^3+1)\}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(The region is shown in <xref ref="fig-case1-region-prefigure"/>. For
each fixed <m>x</m>, <m>y</m> runs from the lower curve to the upper
curve, so integrate along the vertical strips first: write the iterated
integral with its limits, do the inner integral, and then the outer
one.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$\displaystyle\iint_R (2xy+1)\,dx\,dy = \int_{\quad}^{\quad}\!\!\int_{\quad}^{\quad} \cdots$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {inner integral:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {outer integral:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the iterated integral with its limits, for the inner integral, and for the outer integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-case1-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case1-vertical"/>, bounded
above by <m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m> and below by
<m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>. For each fixed
<m>x</m>, the inner integral runs along a vertical strip from the
lower curve to the upper curve.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A region between an upper and a lower curve, with vertical strips.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case1-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>f1(t) = (t, t^3 + 1)</definition>
<definition>f2(t) = (t, -2*t^2 + 2*t)</definition>
<definition>f1sc(t) = t^3 + 1</definition>
<definition>f2sc(t) = -2*t^2 + 2*t</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.4,1.45,2.45)">
<axes at="c1-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="c1-region" function1="f2sc" function2="f1sc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#d3e8d3" stroke="none"/>
<line at="c1-strip1" endpoints="((0.25,0.375),(0.25,1.0156))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-strip2" endpoints="((0.5,0.5),(0.5,1.125))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-strip3" endpoints="((0.75,0.375),(0.75,1.4219))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="c1-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<line at="c1-right" endpoints="((1,0),(1,2))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f1" function="f1" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f2" function="f2" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="c1-f1-label" anchor="(0.42,1.35)" alignment="nw"
    color="#0072B2"><m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m></label>
<label at="c1-f2-label" anchor="(0.52,0.28)" alignment="s"
    color="#D55E00"><m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m></label>
<point at="c1-x0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</point>
<point at="c1-x1" p="(1,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>1</m>
</point>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The region between two curves above the interval from zero to one on the x axis. The upper boundary, drawn in blue, is f 1 of x equals x cubed plus one, rising from one to two. The lower boundary, drawn in vermillion, is f 2 of x equals negative two x squared plus two x, an arch from zero up to one half and back to zero. The region between them is shaded light green with three vertical green strips.">
  <annotation ref="c1-f1" text="The upper boundary f 1 of x equals x cubed plus 1, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-f2" text="The lower boundary f 2 of x equals negative 2 x squared plus 2 x, drawn in vermillion."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-region" text="The region R, shaded in light green."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-strip2" text="A typical vertical strip at a fixed x. The inner integral in y runs along this strip from the lower curve to the upper curve."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case2">
<title>Case II: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : c \le y \le d,\ g_1(y) \le x \le g_2(y)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case2-horizontal">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R x^2 y\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0.1 \le y \le 0.5,\ y^2 \le x \le y^{1/3}\}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(The region is shown in <xref ref="fig-case2-region-prefigure"/>. This
time, for each fixed <m>y</m>, <m>x</m> runs along a horizontal strip
from the left curve to the right curve, so integrate with respect to
<m>x</m> first.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {$\displaystyle\iint_R x^2 y\,dx\,dy = \int_{\quad}^{\quad}\!\!\int_{\quad}^{\quad} \cdots$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {inner integral:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {outer integral:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the iterated integral with its limits, for the inner integral, and for the outer integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-case2-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case2-horizontal"/>, bounded
on the left by <m>g_1(y) = y^2</m> and on the right by
<m>g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m> for <m>0.1 \le y \le 0.5</m>. For each fixed
<m>y</m>, the inner integral runs along a horizontal strip from the
left curve to the right curve.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A region between a left and a right curve, with horizontal strips.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case2-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>g1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>g2(t) = (t^(1/3), t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.12,-0.09,1.22,0.68)">
<axes at="c2-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<polygon at="c2-region" fill="#d3e8d3" stroke="none" closed="yes"
    points="((0.01,0.1),(0.01361,0.1167),(0.01778,0.1333),(0.0225,0.15),(0.02778,0.1667),(0.03361,0.1833),(0.04,0.2),(0.04694,0.2167),(0.05444,0.2333),(0.0625,0.25),(0.07111,0.2667),(0.08028,0.2833),(0.09,0.3),(0.1003,0.3167),(0.1111,0.3333),(0.1225,0.35),(0.1344,0.3667),(0.1469,0.3833),(0.16,0.4),(0.1736,0.4167),(0.1878,0.4333),(0.2025,0.45),(0.2178,0.4667),(0.2336,0.4833),(0.25,0.5),(0.7937,0.5),(0.7848,0.4833),(0.7757,0.4667),(0.7663,0.45),(0.7567,0.4333),(0.7469,0.4167),(0.7368,0.4),(0.7264,0.3833),(0.7157,0.3667),(0.7047,0.35),(0.6934,0.3333),(0.6816,0.3167),(0.6694,0.3),(0.6568,0.2833),(0.6437,0.2667),(0.63,0.25),(0.6156,0.2333),(0.6006,0.2167),(0.5848,0.2),(0.5681,0.1833),(0.5503,0.1667),(0.5313,0.15),(0.5109,0.1333),(0.4886,0.1167),(0.4642,0.1))"/>
<line at="c2-strip1" endpoints="((0.04,0.2),(0.585,0.2))"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
