]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid, marked at one half and one on each axis, for sketching the base of the tetrahedron and a vertical strip.</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>

<remark xml:id="remark-reverse-order">
<title>Why not integrate in the reverse order?</title>
<p>
We could also try to integrate with respect to <m>x</m> first, using
horizontal strips. However, as the figure below shows, the right end of
a horizontal strip depends on where the strip is: for
<m>y \le \tfrac12</m> the strip ends on the line <m>x = 2y</m>, while
for <m>y \ge \tfrac12</m> it ends on the line <m>x + 2y = 2</m> (that
is, <m>x = 2 - 2y</m>). We would therefore have to split the region
into two pieces and evaluate two double integrals, so integrating with
respect to <m>y</m> first is the more efficient choice here.
</p>

<sidebyside width="60%">
<image>
<shortdescription>
The same triangle swept by horizontal arrows for the reversed order of integration.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tetrahedron-base-reverse">
<diagram dimensions="(420,300)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (t, 1 - t/2)</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (t, t/2)</definition>
<definition>topsc(t) = 1 - t/2</definition>
<definition>botsc(t) = t/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.4,2.3,1.55)">
<axes at="rv-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="rv-region" function1="botsc" function2="topsc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
<vector at="rv-strip1" v="(1.35,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.3)" stroke="#D55E00"
    thickness="3"/>
<vector at="rv-strip2" v="(1.25,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.75)" stroke="#CC79A7"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-split" endpoints="((0,0.5),(1.15,0.5))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-top" function="top" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-bot" function="bot" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="#555555"
    thickness="3"/>
<point at="rv-end1" p="(0.6,0.3)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rv-end2" p="(0.5,0.75)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="rv-corner" p="(1,0.5)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac12\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="rv-split-label" anchor="(0.06,0.47)" alignment="se">
<m>y = \tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="rv-top-label" anchor="(0.02,1.18)" alignment="ne"
    color="#0072B2"><m>x = 2 - 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-bot-label" anchor="(0.72,0.2)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>x = 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-origin" anchor="(-0.06,-0.06)" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same triangular region with two horizontal arrows. A vermillion arrow at height y equals zero point three crosses the region and its right end lies on the green line x equals two y. A reddish purple arrow at height y equals zero point seven five crosses the region and its right end lies on the blue line x equals two minus two y. A dashed line at y equals one half separates the two cases.">
  <annotation ref="rv-strip1" text="A horizontal strip below y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-strip2" text="A horizontal strip above y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 minus 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-split" text="The dashed line y equals one half where the right boundary changes from one line to the other, forcing a split into two integrals."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</remark>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-area-by-double-integration">
<title>Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
In the previous session, we talked about finding the double integral
<xref ref="eq-double-integral-def"/> and how it corresponds to the
volume of an object with base <m>R</m> and height <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
This session, we will look at a special case of this double integral,
which corresponds to the area of region <m>R</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-area-double-integral">
\text{Area of region } R = \iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_R dA.
</men>

<example xml:id="example-area-double-integration">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the area of the region <m>R</m> shown in
<xref ref="fig-area-region-prefigure"/>, bounded on the right by
<m>x = y - y^3</m>, on the left by <m>x = -1</m>, and by the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(<alert>Method 1.</alert> Split <m>R</m> at the <m>x</m>-axis into
<m>R_1</m> below and <m>R_2</m> above, as in
<xref ref="fig-area-region-prefigure"/>, and describe each with
horizontal strips; on <m>R_2</m> the left boundary is the parabola,
solved for <m>x</m>. Then the area is
<xref ref="eq-area-double-integral"/> over each piece; the two outer
integrals can be combined before evaluating.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$R_1 =$ \qquad\qquad $R_2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {Area $= \displaystyle\iint_{R_1} dx\,dy + \iint_{R_2} dx\,dy =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the descriptions of the two subregions and one for the area computed as the sum of two iterated integrals.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
(<alert>Method 2, using symmetry.</alert> The curve <m>x = y - y^3</m>
is odd, so rotating it by <m>180^\circ</m> about the origin maps it
onto itself. In <xref ref="fig-area-symmetry-prefigure"/>, which piece
does that rotation carry onto which? Swap them, so that the region
becomes the unit square <m>[-1,0]\times[-1,0]</m> together with the
strip between the parabola and the <m>y</m>-axis, and add the two
areas.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {why the two pieces $A$ have the same area:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {Area $=$ square $+$ strip $=$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the symmetry argument and one for the area as the square plus the strip.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-area-symmetry-prefigure">
<caption>
Method 2: since <m>x = y - y^3</m> is odd, the piece <m>A</m> removed
from the unit square (below the <m>x</m>-axis) is congruent to the
piece <m>A</m> added to the right of the <m>y</m>-axis (above it).
The area is therefore the area of the square plus the area of the green
strip between the parabola <m>y = (x+1)^2</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
The region decomposed into a unit square and two matching pieces.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-area-symmetry">
