<subsection xml:id="subsec-general-regions-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Both problems below ask only for a <em>setup</em>: a double integral whose
value is the volume or the area in question, with a region of integration
that is no longer a rectangle. Each figure can be rotated by dragging the
background, and zoomed by scrolling.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-cylinder-sphere">
<title>A ball cut by a cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the volume of the region bounded by
the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m> and the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>. See <xref ref="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere"/>.
</p>
<p>
(The solid is the part of the ball inside the cylinder: what are its
floor and ceiling, and so its height over a point of the shadow? The
region of integration is the shadow of the solid, the set of points
inside both circles <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> and <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>; find
where the circles cross, and describe the region as in
<xref ref="sec-general-regions-case1"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {height of the solid over $(x,y)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {the circles cross at: \qquad so $D = \{(x,y) :$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$V = \displaystyle\iint_D \cdots\, dA =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the height of the solid, for the crossing points and the description of the region of integration, and for the double integral with limits.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere">
<caption>
Figure (4): the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> and the cylinder
<m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>. Use this graph to find the region of integration
and the integrand. The wall of the cylinder runs straight down to the
<m>xy</m>-plane, where the red circle outlines the region of
integration.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="z7qbvdyz"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The black
curve is where the sphere and the cylinder meet; the region of
integration is the part of the <m>xy</m>-plane lying inside both the red
circle and the shadow of the sphere.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-area-arcsin">
<title>Problem: an area as a double integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the area of the region bounded by
the blue curve and the red curve, which are graphed in
<xref ref="fig-ws-arcsin-region"/>.
</p>
<p>
(Identify the two curves: the red one as <m>x</m> in terms of
<m>y</m>, and the blue one as an arc of a circle whose radius you can
read off the endpoints. Slice the region horizontally; note that the
right end of a slice changes when <m>y</m> passes <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>,
so two integrals are needed. Set them up using
<xref ref="eq-area-double-integral"/>. Then look at what comes out: one
integrand is odd, and what remains is a familiar area.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {red curve: $x =$ \qquad blue curve: $x^2+y^2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$A = \displaystyle\int\!\!\int dx\,dy + \int\!\!\int dx\,dy =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {what it comes to, and why:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the equations of the two curves, for the area as two iterated integrals, and for the value with its explanation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-arcsin-region">
<caption>
Figure (5): the region bounded by the blue arc of the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}</m> and the red curve
<m>y=\sin^{-1}(x)</m>. The two curves meet at
<m>\left(1,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>, which are opposite ends of a
diameter; the dashed arc is the rest of the circle.
</caption>
<image width="64%">
<shortdescription>
A tilted circle with solid and dashed halves bounding the shaded region.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-arcsin-region">
<diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="10">
<definition>R = sqrt(1 + pi^2/4)</definition>
<definition>arcsine(u) = (sin(u), u)</definition>
<definition>circ(t) = (R*cos(t), R*sin(t))</definition>
